    "['switchport trunk native vlan [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 10', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco \"switchport trunk native vlan 10\" command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 10\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family ethernet-switching native-vlan-id 10\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, you need both commands to achieve the equivalent functionality. The first assigns the interface to the VLAN, and the second specifies it as the native VLAN. The interface naming follows Juniper\\'s convention (ge-1/0/1 for GigabitEthernet1/0/1).', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 10'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family ethernet-switching native-vlan-id 10', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco \"switchport trunk native vlan 10\" command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 10\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family ethernet-switching native-vlan-id 10\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, you need both commands to achieve the equivalent functionality. The first assigns the interface to the VLAN, and the second specifies it as the native VLAN. The interface naming follows Juniper\\'s convention (ge-1/0/1 for GigabitEthernet1/0/1).', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family ethernet-switching native-vlan-id 10'}]]": 1,
    "['switchport trunk allowed vlan [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 100', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 100\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 100\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Juniper, the equivalent of Cisco's `switchport trunk allowed vlan 100` is assigning VLAN membership to the interface under the Ethernet switching configuration.\\n- The `ge-1/0/0` and `ge-1/0/1` correspond to Cisco's `GigabitEthernet1/0/0` and `GigabitEthernet1/0/1` interfaces.\\n- The `unit 0 family ethernet-switching` is required for Layer 2 switching configuration.\\n- The `vlan members 100` specifies which VLANs are allowed on the trunk, equivalent to Cisco's allowed VLAN list.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 100'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 100', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 100\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 100\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Juniper, the equivalent of Cisco's `switchport trunk allowed vlan 100` is assigning VLAN membership to the interface under the Ethernet switching configuration.\\n- The `ge-1/0/0` and `ge-1/0/1` correspond to Cisco's `GigabitEthernet1/0/0` and `GigabitEthernet1/0/1` interfaces.\\n- The `unit 0 family ethernet-switching` is required for Layer 2 switching configuration.\\n- The `vlan members 100` specifies which VLANs are allowed on the trunk, equivalent to Cisco's allowed VLAN list.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 100'}]]": 1
